{"ts":"2026-09-01T11:59:31.592824331Z","session":"db7ba632-660e-41c7-bace-799ed3f6d8a2","tool":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"B=\u003crepo\u003e; cd \"$B\"; S=$(cat /tmp/sumdir); chmod -R u+w \"$S\" 2\u003e/dev/null; rm -rf \"$S\" /tmp/sumdir /tmp/sumfpr beadle-daemon\nmake check \u003e/tmp/mcs.log 2\u003e\u00261; echo \"make check: $?\"; git add examples/recipes/summarize.yaml \u0026\u0026 git commit -q -m \"feat(daemon): add the summarize recipe the shipped planner selects\n\nThe planner in cmd/beadle-daemon/main.go is hardcoded to one rule: a\nmessage matching /(?i)summarize|summary/ calls a command named\n'summarize' with a single arg named 'text'. Neither shipped recipe\nsatisfies that -- sysreport declares no args, docs-ask declares\n'question' -- and ValidateArgs rejects undeclared arg names, so a\nmismatch fails the stage even when the name lines up.\n\nThe practical consequence, which nothing recorded until now: both\nexisting examples can be signed and loaded, and neither can be reached\nby sending mail. There was no recipe an end-to-end demo could use.\n\nVerified against the real tooling and the real rule: signs (21 comments\npreserved), verifies good, loads through LoadCommands with enforcement\non, and a RulePlanner built from main.go's exact rule selects it with\nargs that pass ValidateArgs.\n\nThe file's header explains the coupling and points at beadle-ivtd --\nwhen the rule moves into configuration this stops being special and\nbecomes an ordinary example.\" \u0026\u0026 git log --oneline -1","description":"Clean up, gate, commit","timeout":900000},"tool_input_hash":"6858f406948414a119e1debf26f241fa12a66e0f8115482c042dd5cfc04fd505","tool_input_preview":"{\"command\":\"B=\\u003crepo\\u003e; cd \\\"$B\\\"; S=$(cat /tmp/sumdir); chmod -R u+w \\\"$S\\\" 2\\u003e/dev/null; rm -rf \\\"$S\\\" /tmp/sumdir /tmp/sumfpr beadle-daemon\\nmake check \\u003e/tmp/mcs.log 2\\u003e\\u00261..."}
